<div class="help-container">
    <h1>DriveAge Help & Documentation</h1>

    <!-- Risk Model Section -->
    <div class="help-section" id="risk-model">
        <h2>How DriveAge Assesses Risk</h2>

        <p>Every drive is judged on two independent questions, and the dashboard shows both:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Age / wear band</strong> - how old (hard drives) or how worn (SSDs and NVMe) the drive is.
                For hard drives the bands follow Backblaze's fleet failure rates by age (the "AFR" figures in the labels).
                This is a statistic about drives <em>like</em> yours, not evidence about this one.</li>
            <li><strong>SMART health</strong> - what this drive's own SMART data says: <em>Healthy</em>, <em>Warning</em>
                (worth watching), or <em>Failing</em> (act now). <em>N/A</em> means no SMART data is available.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            The <strong>Risk</strong> badge and row color show the worse of the two. A SMART warning raises a drive to at
            least <em>Elevated</em> and is labelled "Warning (SMART)"; a SMART failure raises it to <em>High</em> and is
            labelled "Failing (SMART)" - so a red row always tells you which kind of red it is. Click any row to see both
            parts, the SMART values and how the replacement estimate was worked out.
        </p>

        <h3>"Replace Now" vs "Due now"</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Replace Now</strong> is reserved for evidence from the drive itself - SMART failed, pending or
            uncorrectable sectors, NVMe media errors, a read-only drive. <strong>Due now</strong> means the drive has reached
            the replacement target age (8 years in conservative mode, 10 in aggressive) or used up its rated endurance:
            a planning milestone, not a sign of failure.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Reallocated Sectors Section -->
    <div class="help-section" id="reallocated-sectors">
        <h2>⚠️ Reallocated Sectors (SMART Attribute ID 5)</h2>

        <h3>What are Reallocated Sectors?</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Reallocated sectors</strong> are bad sectors on your hard drive that the drive has detected and automatically
            remapped to spare sectors. This indicates <strong>physical damage</strong> to the disk platter.
        </p>

        <p>
            When a hard drive reads a sector and detects errors, it moves that sector's data to a reserved spare area
            and marks the bad sector as unusable. This is the drive's built-in self-repair mechanism, but it's a
            <strong>warning sign</strong> that the drive is deteriorating.
        </p>

        <h3>Severity Levels</h3>
        <p>
            What matters most is whether the count is <strong>still growing</strong>. DriveAge remembers each drive's
            counts (in its small cache file on the flash drive) and compares every new reading with that history.
        </p>
        <div class="warning-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-warning">WARNING</span>
            <strong>A few reallocated sectors, not increasing</strong><br>
            1-10 sectors while there is under 30 days of history, or any count up to 100 that has not changed for 30+ days.<br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Back up data, keep an eye on the count<br>
            <strong>Estimated Replacement:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>While the history is new: 3 months (conservative) / 6 months (aggressive)</li>
                <li>Once the count has been stable for 30+ days: 12 months / 24 months</li>
            </ul>
            Many drives keep a small, stable number of reallocated sectors for years.
        </div>

        <div class="critical-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>Increasing - or a large count</strong><br>
            Any increase within the last 30 days (shown as "+N since &lt;date&gt;"), more than 10 sectors with no history yet,
            or more than 100.<br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Replace as soon as possible<br>
            <strong>Estimated Replacement:</strong> Now (0 months)<br><br>
            A rising count means the surface is still deteriorating - the strongest single warning sign.
        </div>

        <h3>Why These Time Estimates?</h3>
        <p>
            The replacement estimates are based on research from <strong>Google's drive reliability study</strong>, which analyzed
            100,000+ drives in data centers. Their research found that SMART attribute 5 (Reallocated Sectors) is one of the
            <strong>strongest predictors of imminent drive failure</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
            According to their data, <strong>ANY</strong> reallocated sectors significantly increase failure risk, and drives were
            about 14 times more likely to fail within 60 days of their <em>first</em> reallocation. That is why a growing count is
            treated as critical, while a count that has stayed put for a month gets a longer planning window.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box">
            <strong>Important:</strong> These estimates are <em>probabilistic guidance</em>, not guarantees. Some drives with
            reallocated sectors continue working for years, while others fail quickly. The plugin uses conservative estimates
            to give you time to plan replacement during a maintenance window.
        </div>

        <h3>What Should You Do?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Back up your data immediately</strong> if you haven't already</li>
            <li><strong>Monitor the drive</strong> - if the reallocated sector count increases, failure is imminent</li>
            <li><strong>Plan to replace</strong> the drive within the estimated timeframe</li>
            <li><strong>Don't panic</strong> - drives don't always fail immediately, but planning is essential</li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Aggressive mode in <a href="/Settings/DriveAgeSettings">DriveAge Settings</a> doubles these planning windows
            (6 instead of 3 months, 24 instead of 12 once stable).
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Other SMART Warnings Section -->
    <div class="help-section" id="other-smart-warnings">
        <h2>Other HDD SMART Warnings</h2>

        <h3>SMART Overall-Health FAILED</h3>
        <div class="critical-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>Drive self-assessment: FAILED</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Replace immediately<br><br>
            The drive's own firmware reports that its SMART overall-health self-assessment has FAILED.
            This means the drive has crossed a vendor-defined internal threshold and considers itself failing.
            DriveAge marks these drives High Risk and "Replace Now" regardless of age or other attributes.
        </div>

        <h3>Pending Sectors (SMART Attribute ID 197)</h3>
        <div class="critical-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>ANY value > 0</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Replace ASAP<br><br>
            Pending sectors are waiting to be reallocated. The drive is actively failing and attempting to remap bad sectors.
            This indicates imminent failure risk.
        </div>

        <h3>Uncorrectable Sectors (SMART Attribute ID 198)</h3>
        <div class="critical-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>ANY value > 0</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Replace immediately<br><br>
            Uncorrectable errors indicate permanent data loss. Sectors cannot be read or written, and data may already be lost.
            Replace the drive immediately and restore from backups.
        </div>

        <h3>Reported Uncorrectable Errors (SMART Attribute ID 187)</h3>
        <div class="warning-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-warning">WARNING</span>
            <strong>ANY value > 0</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Monitor closely, plan replacement<br><br>
            Read errors during normal operation. The drive is having difficulty reading data but has not yet failed completely.
        </div>

        <h3>Command Timeout (SMART Attribute ID 188)</h3>
        <div class="warning-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-warning">WARNING</span>
            <strong>1-100 timeouts:</strong> Monitor closely<br>
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>>100 timeouts:</strong> Replace ASAP<br><br>
            Commands that timed out indicate communication failures between the drive and system. High timeout counts
            suggest the drive is becoming unresponsive.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- NVMe Warnings Section -->
    <div class="help-section" id="nvme-warnings">
        <h2>NVMe SSD Health Warnings</h2>

        <h3>Media Errors</h3>
        <div class="critical-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>ANY value > 0</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Replace ASAP<br><br>
            NAND chip read/write errors indicate uncorrectable data errors. The SSD's reliability is compromised.
        </div>

        <h3>Critical Warning Flags</h3>
        <div class="critical-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>Spare low (0x01), reliability degraded (0x04), read-only (0x08), backup failed (0x10), PMR read-only (0x20)</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Replace - a read-only drive immediately (copy data off first)<br><br>
            The NVMe Critical Warning field is a set of independent flags. DriveAge decodes each one: these flags mean
            the drive itself is degraded or out of spare capacity.
        </div>
        <div class="warning-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-warning">WARNING</span>
            <strong>Temperature over threshold (0x02)</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Improve cooling<br><br>
            The drive got hotter (or colder) than its limits. This is a cooling problem, not wear - NVMe drives in
            cache pools often trip it during heavy writes. Add airflow or a heatsink; it does not shorten the
            replacement estimate.
        </div>

        <h3>Available Spare Below Threshold</h3>
        <div class="critical-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
            <strong>Spare < Threshold (typically 10%)</strong><br>
            <strong>Action:</strong> Replace now (within a month)<br><br>
            The SSD has consumed most of its spare NAND blocks. When spare capacity is fully depleted, the drive will fail.
        </div>

        <h3>High Percentage Used</h3>
        <div class="warning-box">
            <span class="severity-badge severity-warning">WARNING</span>
            <strong>80-100% used:</strong> Moderate wear band - plan a replacement<br>
            <strong>Over 100% used:</strong> Elevated band, estimate "Due now"<br><br>
            The drive's own wear indicator: how much of its rated endurance has been consumed. Past 100% the
            rated endurance is used up; many drives keep running well beyond it, so this is a planning signal -
            spare capacity and media errors tell you when it is actually wearing out.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Prediction Modes Section -->
    <div class="help-section" id="prediction-modes">
        <h2>Conservative vs Aggressive Prediction Modes</h2>

        <p>
            DriveAge offers two prediction modes that affect how replacement estimates are calculated:
        </p>

        <h3>Conservative Mode (Default)</h3>
        <p><strong>Philosophy:</strong> Build in safety margins; replace before the end-of-life failure ramp</p>
        <ul>
            <li>HDD SMART warnings: <strong>3 months</strong> replacement estimate</li>
            <li>Target HDD age: <strong>8 years</strong> - ahead of the failure-rate spike Backblaze measured near 10 years</li>
            <li>NVMe wear projections: the drive's <strong>own wear indicator (Percentage Used) and measured wear rate +25% safety margin</strong>
                (a 5-year wear life is assumed only when drive history is under ~3 months)</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Production systems, mission-critical data, risk-averse users</p>

        <h3>Aggressive Mode</h3>
        <p><strong>Philosophy:</strong> Ride drives to the measured fleet failure peak; maximize service life</p>
        <ul>
            <li>HDD SMART warnings: <strong>6 months</strong> replacement estimate</li>
            <li>Target HDD age: <strong>10 years</strong> - the AFR peak (~4.25%) in Backblaze's 2025 data</li>
            <li>NVMe wear projections: the drive's <strong>own wear indicator and measured wear rate</strong>, no extra margin
                (a 5-year wear life is assumed only when drive history is under ~3 months)</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Home labs, non-critical storage, budget-conscious users</p>

        <div class="info-box">
            You can change the prediction mode in <a href="/Settings/DriveAgeSettings">DriveAge Settings</a>.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Research Sources Section -->
    <div class="help-section" id="research">
        <h2>Research Sources</h2>

        <p>DriveAge's predictions are based on peer-reviewed research and industry data:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Backblaze Drive Stats - Bathtub Curve Revisited (2025)</strong><br>
                ~317,000 drives in production. Failure rates hold roughly flat (~1-1.5% AFR) through most of
                drive life, then spike near end of life - peaking at <strong>~4.25% AFR at ~10 years 3 months</strong>.
                DriveAge's default risk categories and replacement targets are calibrated to this analysis.<br>
                <a href="https://www.backblaze.com/blog/are-hard-drives-getting-better-lets-revisit-the-bathtub-curve/" target="_blank">backblaze.com - Let's Revisit the Bathtub Curve</a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Backblaze Hard Drive Stats (ongoing quarterly reports)</strong><br>
                <a href="https://www.backblaze.com/blog/backblaze-drive-stats-for-2024/" target="_blank">https://www.backblaze.com/blog/backblaze-drive-stats-for-2024/</a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Google Drive Reliability Study (2007)</strong><br>
                100,000+ drives analyzed, identified SMART attributes 5, 187, 188, 197, 198 as strongest failure
                predictors - all five are evaluated by DriveAge<br>
                <a href="https://research.google/pubs/pub32774/" target="_blank">https://research.google/pubs/pub32774/</a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Consumer SSD Endurance Research</strong><br>
                NVMe drives report Percentage Used - their own estimate of rated endurance consumed - and DriveAge
                projects that first. Vendor ratings for consumer TLC drives typically run 300-600 TBW per TB, so
                500 TBW/TB is used only as a guess for drives that report no wear indicator
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            For detailed methodology, see <code>METHODOLOGY.md</code> in the plugin repository.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <div class="help-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Q: My drive shows red/high risk but has been working fine for months. Is it really going to fail?</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>A:</strong> Not necessarily. Red/high-risk categorization indicates <em>elevated probability</em> of failure,
            not certainty. Some drives run for years beyond their expected lifespan. However, the risk increases with age and
            SMART warnings, so planning proactive replacement is recommended.
        </p>

        <h3>Q: Should I replace a drive immediately if it has reallocated sectors?</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>A:</strong> Not if the count is small and stable - back up your data and plan a replacement (DriveAge shows
            3-6 months while it is still learning the drive's history, 12-24 months once the count has been stable for 30 days).
            If the count is <strong>increasing</strong>, replace the drive as soon as possible.
        </p>

        <h3>Q: Why doesn't my NVMe drive show a replacement estimate?</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>A:</strong> NVMe estimates require either: (1) Percentage Used data, (2) Data Units Written, or (3) Available Spare
            consumption. Very new drives may show "Unknown" until enough data is collected. This is normal. Healthy drives
            with light wear show "10+ years" - projections beyond ten years are not meaningful as dates.
            Drives with less than ~3 months of power-on history use assumed write rates instead of measured ones
            (shown in the estimate's tooltip), so early estimates carry lower confidence.
        </p>

        <h3>Q: Can I trust these predictions?</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>A:</strong> DriveAge's predictions are based on industry research and real-world data from millions of drives.
            However, they are <em>probabilistic estimates</em>, not guarantees. Individual drives may vary. Always maintain backups
            of critical data regardless of predictions.
        </p>

        <h3>Q: Why is my old drive showing as green/low risk?</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>A:</strong> This was a bug in earlier versions! As of Build 21, drives with replacement estimates less than
            6 months are automatically upgraded to high risk (red) regardless of age. If you're seeing this, please update to the
            latest version.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
